Is he still alive, Rabbi Gambach?

01:10:33.040 --> 01:10:35.040
Ninety something.

01:10:35.040 --> 01:10:36.040
He was a wonderful man.

01:10:36.040 --> 01:10:40.040
Mention my name to him, because he was in my home in Brooklyn when he just came from Cuba.

01:10:40.040 --> 01:10:42.040
He's a lovely, sweet...

01:10:42.040 --> 01:10:46.040
He used to come by bus with his wife with bags in his hands,

01:10:46.040 --> 01:10:52.040
because we had one little room that he could sleep in because he didn't travel on Shabbat.

01:10:52.040 --> 01:11:13.020
And he would be Chaham, he would be Hazzan and everything else to us. He was such a wonderful man. And people later on didn't appreciate what a real Rabbi this man was. And knew everything by heart. I mean, he was gentle and sweet and smiled. And his wife, poor thing, when they finally made a trip to Israel, his wife had a stroke and died there.

01:11:13.020 --> 01:11:17.020
I think they brought a bandit guard here.

01:11:17.020 --> 01:11:25.020
But I love that man because he was the epitome of a human being, you know, and a wonderful Rabbi.

01:11:25.020 --> 01:11:30.020
And he had patience with everybody. Patience with everybody.

01:11:30.020 --> 01:11:35.020
And never made it difficult, gave you a simple answer, and not because he didn't know.

01:11:35.020 --> 01:11:38.020
